The program begins by reading the grid dimensions \(N\) and \(M\), the maximum number of moves \(P\), and the starting position \((L,C)\). It then reads the \(N \times M\) matrix of diamond values into a two‑dimensional array. The variable `fmax` is set to the value at the starting cell, and a variable `max` is initialized to the smallest possible integer.

If no moves are allowed (\(P=0\)), the program simply outputs the value of the starting cell. Otherwise it enters a loop that will iterate at most \(P\) times. In each iteration the program looks at four specific neighboring cells relative to the current position:

* `f1` – cell two rows above and one column left (`[L-2][C-1]`);
* `f2` – cell one row above (`[L-1][C]`);
* `f3` – cell one column left (`[L][C-1]`);
* `f4` – cell one row above and two columns left (`[L-1][C-2]`).

It selects the maximum of these four values (`fmax`) and then moves the current position to the cell that contained that maximum, updating `L` and `C` according to which of the four cases was chosen. After the move, if the new `fmax` is larger than the current `max`, it updates `max`. The loop continues until the move counter reaches \(P\) or the position checks in the loop condition fail.

Finally, the program prints the value stored in `max`, which is intended to be the highest diamond value reachable within the allowed number of moves.
